From e874dcb088005050f9e186b2d723e93bc10383d4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Sedgmen <dsedgmen@redhat.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 09:09:40 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 07/28] Add the overlay build, the on-cluster dev loop, and a
 fork README

hack/try-on-cluster.sh carries the working tree into a stock must-gather pod
and runs a collection script from /tmp/better-gather, with no image build or
push. Because every script sources "$(dirname "$0")/common.sh", running them
from there picks up the modified library automatically, which turns a
change-test cycle into about a minute. The scripts are base64'd for the same
reason must-gather-sos does it: oc joins everything after `--` with spaces
and drops the result into an unquoted heredoc, so a plain inline script would
be expanded by the outer shell before the pod ever saw it.

Dockerfile.overlay layers the collection scripts onto an existing must-gather
image rather than building from registry.ci.openshift.org. For testing
against a real cluster that is both simpler and more faithful: point BASE at
the image the cluster already runs and the base is version-matched and
pullable, with the scripts as the only difference from stock.

README documents what differs from upstream and why, with the measurements
behind it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
